A||||7||fierohoho||Fiberglassing my headliner, how many layers?||||1||
Z||000000||fierohoho||11-13-2001||05:00 PM||||I am almost done with the first layer but it still seems pretty rough, more layers should smooth it out but, how many layers have people who have done this put on?<P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lightbar.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||11-14-2001||03:08 AM||||Are you repairing your headliner board or just strengthening it?  If you're repairing it, a couple of layers of mat should be more than enough (on both sides).  If you're just strenthening it, you should have just used resin (no mat).  If you used mat, start sanding it, then apply a coat of resin only.  If you're going to cover it with the foam type headliner material, it doesn't have to be quite as smooth as it would if you're covering it with something thinner.  (I covered mine with good vinyl, but I went with the Fiero Stores [courtesy of Old Lar--thanx] fiberglass board.  Starts out smooth.)||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierohoho||11-14-2001||06:09 AM||||I am just strengthening it as it had a couple small tears/cracks in it when I pulled it down and removed the old liner.<P>When done I will recover it, not sure what I want to use yet.<P>I have put down one layer of resin only which soaked in pretty good leaving a somewhat rough surface, no mat used.<P>As I will probably cover it with something other than the foam backed cloth I was curious as to how many coats/layers to put down, should I sand lightly between coats?<P>Don't think at this point it would be good to sand it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfixit58||11-14-2001||09:25 AM||||My headliner was broken into four pieces. I used fiberglass resin and an office stapler to put it back together and to strengthen the skinny sides (sunroof car). This is all that was required. I used foam backed cloth like the original. <P>If you going to use something else, you can still use a layer of foam to smooth out the rough surface. If you using vinyl, it also comes in a foam back form. I would be afraid the too many layers of fiberglass would make it too think to get a clean installation.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||7
Z||000004||Sage||11-14-2001||10:32 AM||||Just the resin should strengthen and stiffen it enough.  If you are dealing with tears or a broken board, you can just patch the bad spots with 1 layer of 1 1/2 oz. matte with about an inch overlap on the edges, then feather the edges a little bit to blend it in so they don't show through after you cover it.  Or just put it on the backside of the board.  If you want to make it real sturdy, 1 layer of 1 1/2 matte should suffice, or 1 layer of 3/4 oz. on each side.  Just keep in mind that you will make it thicker as you add layers.  Monkeyman's suggestion is probably the best way to go.  Good luck with your project!<P>------------------<BR>Have a good one!:D||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierohoho||11-14-2001||12:29 PM||||Thanks for the help guys, just going for the strength side of this so it will hold up to the handling. <P>I think I will just do the resin on both side of the board and limit it to one layer(of resin, no cloth) on each side, let me know if you think this is too much.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||11-14-2001||09:55 PM||||Personnaly I would use cloth not mat.||||reg||1
Z||000007||tgowens||11-15-2001||01:44 AM||||I've done them all kinds of ways......only if the board is severly cracked will I use cloth or mat..... If it is a clean "break" I just use duct tape to hold the pieces together and resin over it...... the biggest problem areas for breaks are front, back and both sides on a sunroof car and front, back and middle on a non-sunroof car. Even applying resin to the non-covered side will provide enough strength to keep it from breaking -- don't make it too thick or you will have a problem fitting the interior components (visors, third brake light cover, dome light, etc.......). The material I use (foam backed) is slightly thicker than the original fabric too.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature_TGO.jpg"> <P><A HREF="http://www.cfog-i.org" TARGET=_blank>Classic Fiero Owners Group International</A>||||reg||1
